Deploying a Flask app with Waitress is a fantastic choice, especially for Windows, because it is a production-grade WSGI server (unlike the default Flask development server) and runs perfectly natively on Windows (unlike Gunicorn).

Here is the step-by-step guide on how to set it up for your Asset Manager backend:

Step 1: Install Waitress
Open your terminal in the Backend folder (make sure your virtual environment is activated) and run:

bash


pip install waitress
(You can also add waitress==3.0.0 to your requirements.txt file so you don't forget it on the other PC).

Step 2: Create a Production Run Script
The cleanest way to run Waitress is to create a small python script that starts your app.

Create a new file in your Backend folder named run_waitress.py and paste this code into it:

python


from waitress import serve
from app import app  # Imports the Flask 'app' object from your app.py file
if __name__ == '__main__':
    print("Starting Waitress production server on http://0.0.0.0:5000...")
    # serve(app, host, port, threads)
    serve(app, host='0.0.0.0', port=5000, threads=6)
Note: threads=6 allows it to handle 6 requests simultaneously. You can increase this if you expect heavy traffic.

Step 3: Run the Server
Instead of running python app.py (which uses the slow development server), you will now start your backend by running:

bash


python run_waitress.py
Your terminal will show Starting Waitress production server... and your Flask app is now running in production mode!

Step 4 (Optional): Make it run in the background as a Windows Service
If you are deploying this on a server PC and want it to start automatically when the PC turns on (even if nobody is logged in), the industry standard on Windows is to use a free tool called NSSM (Non-Sucking Service Manager).

Download NSSM (from nssm.cc) and extract it.
Open an Administrator Command Prompt and run: nssm.exe install AssetManagerBackend
A GUI window will pop up:
Path: Browse and select the python.exe located inside your Backend\venv\Scripts\ folder.
Arguments: run_waitress.py
Directory: Browse and select your Backend folder.
Click "Install Service".
Now you can open the Windows "Services" app (services.msc), find AssetManagerBackend, and set it to Start Automatically!
